```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>My Joke Website</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            text-align: center;
            margin-top: 50px;
        }
        h1 {
            color: #3399FF;
        }
        .joke-container {
            border: 2px solid black;
            padding: 20px;
        }
        button {
            background-color: #66CCFF;
            color: white;
            padding: 10px 20px;
            cursor: pointer;
            border: none;
            border-radius: 5px;
        }
        button:hover {
            background-color: #4CAF50;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="joke-container">
        <h1>My Joke Website</h1>
        <p id="joke" class="hidden">[Funny Joke]</p>
        <button onclick="showPunchline()">Push to reveal punchline</button>
    </div>

    <script>
        document.getElementById('joke').textContent = "Why do programmers always mix up Christmas and Halloween?";
        function showPunchline() {
            const joke = document.getElementById('joke');
            if (joke.classList.contains('hidden')) {
                joke.classList.remove('hidden');
            } else {
                joke.classList.add('hidden');
            }
        }
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

This code creates a basic webpage with the elements you've sketched:
- It has a header saying "My Joke Website".
- There's an area where a joke is hidden initially.
- A button labeled "push to reveal punchline" allows toggling between hiding or showing the joke.

The joke shown is the classic "Why do programmers always mix up Christmas and Halloween?" which is a humorous play on the similarity of the dates. Adjust the joke string according to your preference.

Remember to save this code in an `.html` file and open it in a web browser to see it in action!
